Clean compile, 7 tests still green.

## Refactor Phase Complete

**Refactoring applied**: replaced `results: unknown[]` with a named `QuoteResult` type.

```ts
export type QuoteResult = {
  premium: number;
};

export type ScenarioResults = {
  results: QuoteResult[];
};
```

**Naming evaluation (done first)**: I checked every name against its current behavior. `runScenario`, `quotePremium`, `policyBasePremium`, `addPercent`, `BASE_PREMIUM_BY_TYPE`, `PROCESSING_FEE`, and `FIRST_INSURANCE_SURCHARGE_PERCENT` all still fit what they do — no rename warranted. `roundPremiumInMHPCOsFavor` I considered for removal since it is a pure one-call alias for `Math.ceil` (mass 5, would drop to 0 if inlined). I kept it: the spec pairs "premiums round up" with "payouts round down" as one domain rule, and the name is what carries that rule. Inlining would trade a domain concept for 5 units of mass — Rule 2 trumps APP and Rule 4 here.

**Why the `unknown[]` was the right target**: it is a Rule 2 defect (the type tells a reader nothing about what a result is) reachable through Rule 4 — `unknown` is an element carrying no information. All 7 passing tests already pin the shape to `{ premium: number }`, so naming it documents no more than the tests already prove. It is not speculative generality: I deliberately named it `QuoteResult` rather than a union covering claims, because no claim test is active yet. When the payout tests land (`payout`/`remainingCap`, line 138 onward), `ScenarioResults["results"]` becomes a union of `QuoteResult | ClaimResult` — that widening is a later refactoring driven by a real test, not now.

**APP mass**: unchanged at the value level — this is a type-level change, and TypeScript types are erased at runtime, so they contribute no runtime mass. The gain is entirely Rule 2 (intent).

**Verification**: `pnpm test` → 7 passed, 43 todo. I also ran `npx tsc --noEmit` separately and it was clean. That second check mattered: vitest transpiles TypeScript without type-checking it, so a green test run would have looked identical even if the new type were wrong. The compiler confirms `runScenario`'s actual return value satisfies `QuoteResult[]` — meaning the type now genuinely constrains the implementation rather than just decorating it.

Files: `/home/experimenter/experiments/runs/2026-09-05_09-40-45_claim-office-example-mapping_v6.1.5-pure-split-cc_opus-5-no-thinking/src/claim-office.ts`

One thing worth flagging for the next Red phase: `policyBasePremium` does an unguarded `BASE_PREMIUM_BY_TYPE[item.type]` lookup, so an unknown type yields `NaN` rather than an error. Test line 146 ("quote with an unknown item type → error") will drive that out — I left it alone rather than implementing ahead of the test.
